session:pr188 a agp:AgentSession ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith session:claudeSonnet5 ;
    prov:used ghissues:174 ;
    prov:startedAtTime "2026-07-19T21:21:09Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-07-19T21:45:09Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

session:pr188Summary a agp:TranscriptSummary ;
    agp:summaryText "Phase C-2 of the join-reordering epic. Closes #174. Follow-up to #38/PR #173, which introduced sparql_parser::component_ordering and treated OPTIONAL/MINUS/FILTER/BIND/SERVICE all as unconditional barriers — nothing was ever reordered across any of them. This extends reordering to OPTIONAL and MINUS specifically (FILTER/BIND/SERVICE stay hard barriers, unchanged, per the issue's scope): a later conjunct that's independent of the barrier's internal-only variables can be hoisted to run before it, shrinking what the OPTIONAL/MINUS has to process. Correctness conditions OPTIONAL — the standard outer-join-pushdown rewrite: `` (Ω1 OPT Ω2) ⋈ Ω3 = (Ω1 ⋈ Ω3) OPT Ω2 iff vars(Ω3) ∩ vars(Ω2) ⊆ vars(Ω1) ` i.e. the later conjunct must not touch a variable bound *exclusively* inside the OPTIONAL body. MINUS — derived independently, not copied from OPTIONAL (SPARQL's MINUS has a domain-disjointness quirk where a row sharing no variable at all with a MINUS solution is never excluded). Turns out this codebase's actual Minus evaluation (eval_component in execute.rs) threads the outer row into the inner body and only ever extends it, so compatible() is effectively always true given a non-empty inner result — the domain-disjointness escape hatch isn't actually exercised, and the current behavior is equivalent to FILTER NOT EXISTS { inner }." ;
    agp:reasoningFor ghpull:188 ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy session:pr188 ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo session:claudeSonnet5 .
